The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if ((ref_count == 0 && ref_change < 0) || (ref_count == U64_MAX && ref_change > 0)) {
    ext4_error_inode(ea_inode, __func__, __LINE__, 0,
        "EA inode %lu ref wraparound: ref_count=%lld ref_change=%d",
        ea_inode->i_ino, ref_count, ref_change);
    ret = -EFSCORRUPTED;
    goto out;
}
```
Reason: The `if` block detects a wraparound condition and sets `ret = -EFSCORRUPTED` before jumping to the `out` label. However, before this check, the function successfully called `ext4_reserve_inode_write(handle, ea_inode, &iloc)`, which allocates a buffer head (`iloc.bh`). The `out` label only releases the inode lock but does not call `brelse(iloc.bh)`, causing a buffer head leak. This leak occurs because the error path omits the necessary cleanup that would normally happen later (e.g., in `ext4_mark_iloc_dirty` or a `brelse` call after the check).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if ((ref_count == 0 && ref_change < 0) || (ref_count == U64_MAX && ref_change > 0)) {
    ext4_error_inode(ea_inode, __func__, __LINE__, 0,
        "EA inode %lu ref wraparound: ref_count=%lld ref_change=%d",
        ea_inode->i_ino, ref_count, ref_change);
+   brelse(iloc.bh);
    ret = -EFSCORRUPTED;
    goto out;
}
```
Reason: The added `brelse(iloc.bh)` releases the buffer head that was acquired by `ext4_reserve_inode_write` before the error return. This ensures that the buffer head reference count is properly decremented, preventing a memory leak. The cleanup is correct because the buffer head is no longer needed once the error is detected and the function must return without marking the inode dirty or performing any further I/O.

